The pH of a person's blood can be found using the formula
\[
\mathrm{pH}=6.1+\log \left(\frac{B}{C}\right)
\]
where \(B\) represents the concentration of bicarbonate in the blood, in \(\mathrm{mEq} / \mathrm{L}\)
\(C\) represents the concentration of carbonic acid in the blood, in \(\mathrm{mEq} / \mathrm{L}\)

Kansas has a blood pH of 7.41. In her blood sample, the concentration of carbonic acid is 1.41 mEq/L. Determine the concentration of bicarbonate in her blood.

Express the answer correct to one decimal place.

\section*{Solution}

Method 1
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
7.41=6.1+\log \left(\frac{B}{1.41}\right) & 1 / 2 \text { mark for substitution } \\
1.31=\log \left(\frac{B}{1.41}\right) & \\
10^{1.31}=\frac{B}{1.41} & 1 \text { mark for exponential form } \\
B=28.788505 \ldots & \\
B=28.8 \mathrm{mEq} / \mathrm{L} & 1 / 2 \text { mark for solving for } B
\end{array}
\]

2 marks
Method 2
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
7.41=6.1+\log \left(\frac{B}{1.41}\right) & 1 / 2 \text { mark for substitution } \\
1.31=\log \left(\frac{B}{1.41}\right) & \\
1.31=\log B-\log 1.41 & 1 \text { mark for quotient law } \\
1.31+\log 1.41=\log B & \\
1.4592 \ldots=\log B & \\
B=28.788505 \ldots & \\
B=28.8 \mathrm{mEq} / \mathrm{L} & 1 / 2 \text { mark for solving for } B \\
& 2 \text { marks }
\end{array}
\]

