Justify that \(f(x)=3 x+2\) and \(g(x)=\frac{x-3}{2}\) are not inverses of each other.

\section*{Solution}

Method 1
Let \(y=f(x)\)
\[
y=3 x+2
\]
Let \(y=f^{-1}(x)\)
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x=3 y+2 & 1 \text { mark for switching } x \text { and } y \\
\frac{x-2}{3}=y & \\
\frac{x-2}{3}=f^{-1}(x) & 1 \text { mark for justification } \\
g(x) \neq f^{-1}(x) &
\end{array}
\]

2 marks

Method 2
If they are inverses, then \(g(f(x))=x\), and \(f(g(x))=x\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
g(3 x+2) & =\frac{3 x+2-3}{2} \\
& =\frac{3 x-1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
1 mark for \(g(f(x))\) or \(f(g(x))\)
Since \(g(f(x)) \neq x\), they are not inverses of each other. 1 mark for justification

2 marks

The terminal arm of an angle, \(\theta\), in standard position passes through the point \((-3,6)\) and intersects the unit circle at the point \(P(\theta)\). Determine the coordinates of the point \(P(\theta)\).

\section*{Solution}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2} & \\
(-3)^{2}+(6)^{2}=r^{2} & 1 / 2 \text { mark for substitution } \\
9+36=r^{2} & \\
45=r^{2} & \\
\sqrt{45}=r & 1 / 2 \text { mark for solving for } r \\
P(\theta)=\left(\frac{-3}{\sqrt{45}}, \frac{6}{\sqrt{45}}\right) & \begin{array}{c}
1 \text { mark for the coordinates of } P(\theta) \\
(1 / 2 \text { mark for } x \text {-coordinate } ; 1 / 2 \text { mark for } y \text {-coordinate })
\end{array} \\
\quad \text { or } & 2 \text { marks } \\
P(\theta)=\left(\frac{-1}{\sqrt{5}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\right) &
\end{array}
\]
or
\[
P(\theta)=\left(\frac{-\sqrt{5}}{5}, \frac{2 \sqrt{5}}{5}\right)
\]

Determine the exact value of \(2 \sin \theta \cos \theta\) if \(\theta=\frac{\pi}{12}\).

\section*{Solution}
\[
\begin{array}{rlrl}
2 \sin \theta \cos \theta & =\sin 2 \theta & & 1 \text { mark for substitution into correct identity } \\
& =\sin \left(2\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\right) & \\
& =\sin \left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right) & & \\
& =\frac{1}{2} & & 1 \text { mark for consistent value }
\end{array}
\]